  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「オオオニバス」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%82%AA%E3%82%AA%E3%82%AA%E3%83%8B%E3%83%90%E3%82%B9",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 111299319,
      "text": "オオオニバス は、スイレン科オオオニバス属に属する水生植物の1種である。直径2メートル (m) 以上になる巨大な浮水葉を水面に浮かべることで知られている（図1）。南米のアマゾン川流域に生育し、植物園などで栽培されることもある。属名の Victoria はイギリスのビクトリア女王に、種小名の amazonica はその生育地にちなんで命名された。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Victoria amazonica」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victoria_amazonica",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1374550559,
      "text": "Victoria amazonica is a species of flowering plant, the second largest in the water lily family Nymphaeaceae. It is called Vitória-Régia or Iaupê-Jaçanã in Brazil and Atun Sisac in Inca (Quechua). Its native region is tropical South America, specifically Guyana and the Amazon Basin."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Victoria amazonica」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victoria_amazonica#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1374550559,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "V. amazonica is cultivated in botanical gardens worldwide, requiring warm water temperatures of 29–32°C (85–90°F) for optimal growth; mature plants tolerate slightly cooler conditions (24–27°C or 75–80°F). Propagation is primarily by seed, which should be stored submerged in water and scarified to accelerate germination. Rhizome division is also possible during the growing season. In temperate regions, it is grown as an annual in containers with organically rich loam substrate in still water 1–3 m deep.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
